WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:01.260
NATAN YELLIN: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.230
I'm Natan, and today, I'm going
to talk about SRE agents and how

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.110
to build affordable SRE agents
that you can actually run

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.700
on all your production alerts.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.970
So it seems like everyone's
building SRE agents nowadays,

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:16.350
and the math on them is
actually fairly challenging

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.180
to make this work at scale.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.190
So I'm going to walk through
some of that and why they cost

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.530
so much and what you
can do to fix that.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.500
As background, I'm the CEO
and co-founder of Robusta.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.020
So we've been building SRE
agents and shipping them

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.150
to customers from the
Fortune 500 all the way

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.850
to tiny, tiny startups.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:34.590
And we've been doing that
now for quite some time.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.060
So I'm going to share
some of the lessons

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.080
that we've learned on that.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.760
Okay. So the naive cost, if we
take a typical Fortune 500,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.800
you're going to have at least
a thousand alerts per day

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:47.240
across different teams.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:51.540
And if you want to run an AI
SRE on all of those alerts,

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.690
the total cost on that with a
naive solution is just too high.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.250
I'm going to walk through all
the numbers and how I get

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.910
to this giant figure here
for $730,000 a year,

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.130
but it's fairly realistic.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.530
And it matches what
we tend to see

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.020
in enterprise after enterprise.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.320
And the math behind
this is you're looking

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.640
at a cost per investigation
of around $2,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.900
and you're looking at, say,
a thousand alerts per day.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.670
And if you run the
math on that, I mean,

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.850
you very rapidly reach
this massive figure.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.210
So I'm going to walk through all
those numbers in more detail.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.140
And, of course, this number

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.640
for most enterprises is more
expensive than the cost

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.460
of having humans triage
alerts manually.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.320
So it tends to be a non-starter.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.680
So we're going to look at how
you can bring that cost down.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.690
So just to walk through
more of the math.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:49.140
So the typical cost for most
vendors doing SRE agent

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.560
investigations is in the
50-cent to $5 range,

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.410
depending on how complex
the underlying issue is.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.420
Most of the vendors are
charging a markup on that.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.820
So most of the observability
vendors, for example,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.780
are charging around
$25 per investigation.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.080
And then you have vendors
going all the way down to $15

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.300
or $5 per investigation.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.790
But if you ignore for a
second the vendor markup,

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:17.460
then just the underlying cost on
this tends to be 50 cents to $5

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.570
for surprisingly almost
everyone across the industry,

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:24.450
where that's driven by
how complex the alert is.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.990
So a simple investigation that's
pulling relatively little data

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:31.560
would be 50 cents, complex
investigation goes up to $5.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.510
And very interestingly,
these are all driven

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.340
by the cost on input tokens.

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.360
So like an SRE agent is pulling
a ton of logs, ton of metrics,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.900
ton of observability data, you
pay a lot on the input token.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.450
You tend to not pay at
all on output tokens.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.520
Or, I mean, you pay something,
but it tends to be very,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.060
very minimal in what you
pay on output tokens.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:56.670
Now, if we look at this, then
just taking these two ranges,

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.760
you get this range of
180k to 1.8 million,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.770
depending on the cost
per investigation.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.920
And taking a midpoint
there of around $2,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.170
then you get to that $730k.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.930
And these numbers are,
for most companies,

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.270
non-starters at that midpoint.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.640
It's just not -- it doesn't
really pay to do it

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.750
at that range, even though
you're getting like faster MTTR.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.220
It's cheaper to hire
an offshore team

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.110
and to have them
triage the alerts.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:23.550
So we want to play
with this math,

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:26.160
and we want to make
that more attractive.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.840
One thing you can do, it doesn't
help as much as people think,

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.380
but one thing you can do
is to use cheaper models.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.470
So all this assumes Opus 4.7.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.950
And Opus 4.7 is
fantastic, or 4.6, right?

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.660
Really fantastic model.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.630
If you look at the Chinese
models and you look at models

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.590
like DeepSeek V4, then
that's actually nearly three

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:50.790
times cheaper.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:52.260
They're not as good.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.260
We see accuracy does go down
on the really complex things,

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.180
but they're fairly decent.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.060
The math on this is
still not good enough

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:01.980
to really get you
where you need to be.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.920
And what we do, we
run with customers.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.260
We actually don't use
the Chinese models.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:12.780
We use almost exclusively Opus
family models because they give

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:14.640
that extra result and
because we can get the cost

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.280
down in other ways.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:18.360
But if you do look at this,

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.130
this is like a first
really optimization,

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:22.480
just take a cheaper
model, right?

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.210
So one other interesting,
non-intuitive part

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:29.390
on this is actually sometimes
the cheaper models,

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:31.760
even though the cost per
token is going down,

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.750
what we see repeatedly
in benchmarks is

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.420
that the overall price
doesn't go down by as much

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.210
because the models tend to
have to pull more data.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.350
They're like trying
to pull logs,

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.030
but they'll pull the
wrong logs first.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:42.870
So then they have to try again.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.400
So they end up actually
using more tokens,

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.580
even though the cost
per token is cheaper.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:48.870
Okay. Other optimizations.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.660
So we spoke about switching
to a cheaper model.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:56.040
And then one other really common
optimization is doing context

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:56.820
and runbooks.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.610
So if you look at an SRE agent,

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:04.770
an SRE agent is
investigating an alert.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.040
And in a simple architecture
with no memory and no learning

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.640
across investigations, then
you need to rediscover

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.470
in every alert
(inaudible) information

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.740
about the customer environment
and their observability data,

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:21.240
and you have to rediscover
how do you actually query

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:21.660
their tools.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.270
What data lives in different
observability vendors?

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:27.000
If you have multiple time series
database -- one for the infra,

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.040
one for the applications
-- what lives where?

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.080
So you end up having to
rediscover that context

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.340
in each investigation.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.250
So if you either give the
agent context on its own,

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:38.100
or you have it auto-learn that
context and generate memories

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.770
or auto-build skills, then
you can actually get the cost

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:43.950
on that down by quite a bit,

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:48.030
typically as in the
20 to 30% range.

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:51.390
And you can get that down
because the agent doesn't have

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.460
to relearn everything
in each investigation.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.190
So that's another
good optimization.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:59.700
But the big, big area where we
see the biggest optimization is

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.710
that most alerts are
actually duplicates.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:04.770
So if you look at 500
alerts that are coming

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:07.230
in when there's a big
production outage,

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:10.830
most of those are actually
really duplicates

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:12.150
of the same cause.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.220
So if you're running
investigations naively

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:15.930
on each individual alert,

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.420
then you're just paying
a linear cost on that.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.490
But if you're able
to do grouping,

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:24.300
then you're actually able to
reduce that down dramatically.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.230
Now, there's a lot of different
ways that you can do grouping.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.540
And the tricky part is
to do grouping with LLM,

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.900
to do grouping that's LLM-native
as opposed to doing --

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.100
you don't want to do grouping

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.460
with old-school
deterministic rules.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.920
Because if you do that, then
you're losing the whole benefit

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.720
of the AI SRE agent,
and then you end

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:44.700
up grouping either
too aggressively

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.770
where you're grouping
together different things

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.630
that appear the same, but
they're actually different

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:50.280
and have different root causes,

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.230
or you're not grouping
aggressively enough

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:53.550
because you have different
alerts that are coming

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:57.000
in with different names and from
different observability systems,

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.530
and you'd want to group them,

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.480
but they don't match the
deterministic rules.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:04.140
So what we do, for example,
is LLM-native grouping.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:07.200
So we essentially let the LLM
generate the grouping rules

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:09.780
and then persist those for
a certain period of time.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:12.810
And then we do a bunch of other
optimizations on top of that.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.750
But the core idea is to let
the LLM actually control the

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:19.020
grouping mechanism and then to
let the LLM control the policy

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.180
on what triggers
reinvestigations

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:22.930
and what does not.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.470
There are other optimizations
also that tie into this.

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:30.930
So, for example, if
you're doing it this way,

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.500
then you really have one
context window per root cause.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.270
And then even when
new information comes

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:39.120
in on a different alert that
retriggers an investigation,

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.910
you can reuse that
previous context window.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.040
And if that context window
is hot, meaning it's still

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:48.030
in Anthropic's cache, Anthropic
has a five-minute cache

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:50.850
hierarchy, so if the context
window is still hot,

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:51.660
then that actually is way,

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.180
way cheaper than doing
a clean context window.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.360
So there are lots and lots
of associated optimizations

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.380
that you can do around this.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.400
When you start to apply these,

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:05.730
then you can actually get
the numbers down to a very,

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:07.350
very reasonable range.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.390
And the outcome of this is

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:12.030
that you can run AI
SRE investigations

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:13.080
on every single alert.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:16.230
So when you're at $5 per
investigation, now you have

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:17.940
to start being really
selective and choose

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:19.800
where you investigate
and where you don't.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:23.280
When you can get the
per-investigation cost down,

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:24.390
then it's a no-brainer.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:25.860
You just run it on everything.

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:29.100
And then the agent can then
go and automatically escalate

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.110
to human when there's something
that's really important

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:34.020
but initially looks like
a low-priority alert.

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.480
So there are all these benefits
if you can actually get this

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:39.810
in the range where you can
run it on every single alert

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.400
because now you
can drive outcomes

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:42.600
like having the AI do all sorts

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:46.800
of predictive stuff before
problems become P1s.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.630
Okay. So just to wrap up, AI
SREs are extremely accurate.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.430
We see that again and again.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:56.940
But they can be extremely
expensive, even more expensive

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:58.740
than having humans triage.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:02.100
So to make this economic
and to run this at scale,

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.160
you really have to start to
apply those optimizations.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.170
And if you're interested,
please reach out to me.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.660
My email is here, and
I'd love to talk to you

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:12.090
about how we do this and
how you can apply this

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:13.840
to your environment.
